localFidelity.rawSourceSolution:
The logarithmic function $y=\log_e(x-3)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\log_e(x)$ to the right by $3$ units. This shifts the vertical asymptote from $x=0$ to $x=3$.
To find the $x$-intercept, set $y=0$:
$$\begin{align*}
\log_e(x-3) &= 0 \\
x - 3 &= e^0 \\
x - 3 &= 1 \\
x &= 4
\end{align*}$$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (3,-3) -- (3,3) node[above left] {$x=3$};
\draw[domain=3.05:6, samples=100, smooth, thick] plot (\x, {ln(\x-3)});
\fill[black] (4,0) circle (0.08) node[below right] {$4$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

